This guide walks through cutting a Trellis release from the repo: changelog prep, Rust xtask release tooling, the GitHub release gate, CI-created tagging, and publication.

This guide owns Trellis testing and release practice. Rust `xtask release ...` commands are the current release tooling.

## What you need

- push access to the repository
- [Deno](https://deno.com) v2+
- a Rust toolchain (`rustup`)
- Docker or Podman only if you want to locally verify image builds; the live
  integration gate runs NATS as a managed binary and needs no container runtime

## Feature testing policy

Every feature, bug fix, or refactor must explicitly ask which live integration
coverage proves it. Trellis runtime-observable behavior belongs in live
TypeScript and Rust integration tests.

Default to adding or updating integration tests when a change affects:

- public Trellis HTTP or RPC APIs
- NATS subjects, auth-callout, proofs, or transport permissions
- contracts, generated SDKs, manifests, or permission derivation
- service bootstrap, device activation, participant installation, or GrantBinding mutation
- jobs, operations, events, live observations, state, resources, or transfer behavior
- Rust/TypeScript cross-runtime behavior

Unit tests are the exception. Keep them only for code that is not Trellis runtime
behavior: pure parser, codec, canonicalization, crypto vector, schema pointer,
error serialization, package/export/import, generated artifact, CLI planning, or
tiny UI helper checks. Do not use fake NATS, fake Hono, fake storage, fake
runtime, fake auth, or fake generated-client workflows to prove Trellis behavior.
Use real SQLite component tests for deterministic transaction invariants; do not
add synthetic failure controls to the runtime for tests.

Before committing or ending an implementation cycle, run the focused checks that match the files touched. Build the server/CLI and set their paths as described in `integration/README.md`. Each live test owns its real runtime lifecycle:

```sh
cargo test --manifest-path Cargo.toml -p trellis-rs --features live-integration --test integration -- --test-threads=1
deno test -A -c ts/integration/deno.json ts/integration
```

Cargo and Deno discover executable tests directly. Hidden skips and separate
test registries are forbidden.

If the full integration suites cannot be run, record the reason and the last focused checks that did run.

### Targeted integration loops

Executable Rust and Deno tests are the integration catalog.

For focused local debugging, run the smallest matching Rust test or Deno test file directly:

```sh
cargo test --manifest-path Cargo.toml -p trellis-rs --features live-integration --test integration
deno test -A -c ts/integration/deno.json --filter 'generated runtime workflows' ts/integration
```

Run both suites before accepting changes to transport or runtime behavior:

```sh
cargo test --manifest-path Cargo.toml -p trellis-rs --features live-integration --test integration -- --test-threads=1
deno test -A -c ts/integration/deno.json ts/integration
```

For focused Rust diagnostics, pass a native test-filter substring to Cargo.

## Formatting policy

Formatting is part of the normal implementation loop, not just release verification. After editing source, docs, styles, generated-artifact inputs, or workflow files, format the changed files before running type checks and tests.

Use targeted formatting for the files changed by the task:

```sh
deno fmt -c ts/deno.json <changed ts/ts/svelte/json/md/css/svg files>
cargo fmt --manifest-path Cargo.toml --package <crate>
rustfmt --edition 2021 <changed .rs files>
```

`xtask` is a standalone Rust workspace. When a change touches it, run its formatter check directly:

```sh
cargo fmt --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml --check
```

When generated artifacts are affected, run `cargo xtask install` first, then verify generated Rust formatting with:

```sh
cargo fmt --manifest-path Cargo.toml --all --check
```

If a full formatter check reports unrelated pre-existing drift, keep the task patch scoped and report that drift separately unless the cleanup is explicitly part of the task.

## Release model

Trellis has one release tag for the whole repo, for example `vX.Y.Z` or `vX.Y.Z-rc.1`.

That single tag drives:

- GitHub release assets
- npm package publication
- Rust crate publication
- OCI image publication

JSR publication is enabled for the publishable package set.

Release-managed TypeScript packages and Rust crates use one checked-in base version. Stable releases usually bump that checked-in base version. Prerelease tags use a suffix such as `-rc.1`; they normally keep the checked-in base version unchanged.

The GitHub `Release` workflow runs the same Rust release tooling used locally:

- `release prepare --tag <tag>` rewrites publishable versions for the tagged build
- `release check-metadata` checks release metadata before expensive verification
- `release write-notes` extracts the matching `CHANGELOG.md` section for GitHub release notes

The normal release path is a `release/vX.Y.Z` or `release/vX.Y.Z-rc.1` branch marker. Release preparation (the version rewrite and the `CHANGELOG.md` section) is committed to the candidate before it is verified: `release prepare --tag` is run and committed, so `Check` verifies exactly what ships. `Check` is the single build-and-verification pipeline. It runs on `main`, on pull requests, and on `release/*` pushes; builds the release-profile runtime once and reuses it for live, browser, console, and demo tests; builds every supported CLI target and assembles and smokes both image architectures; packages the Rust and npm packages; runs the artifact-only package consumer in full mode; and reports through one acceptance gate. Formatting, lint/type checks, library tests, generated freshness, and live integration are part of that same graph. `Release` is publication only: it selects the successful `Check` run for the exact candidate commit (never "the latest green Check"), downloads that run's verified artifacts, and publishes crates, npm, JSR, canonical APIs, and images without rebuilding the product. After publication gates pass, GitHub Actions creates an unsigned lightweight release tag on the candidate commit. If verification fails, no release tag is created and the same version can be retried.

The `Deploy Site to GitHub Pages` workflow is separate from release publishing. It installs each worktree's locked APIs from source before building the released and current guide and Console sites.

The JSR publish set is:

- `@oatscenter/result`
- `@oatscenter/trellis`
- `@oatscenter/trellis-test`
- `@oatscenter/trellis-svelte` from `ts/packages/trellis-svelte/jsr`

The workflow runs JSR dry-runs for the publishable core packages before the release gate. The source `@oatscenter/trellis-svelte` package is built and smoke-tested before release, then staged and published after the matching core JSR version is available; a prepublication JSR dry-run cannot resolve that unreleased dependency.

The published Rust crate set is:

- `trellis-protocol`
- `trellis-rs`
- `trellis-test`

`release prepare` also rewrites the external testkit fixture manifests under
`integration/fixtures/testkit` so its generated application package carries the
prepared version. The `rust-testkit-consumer` release job builds a verification
bundle (the three `.crate` archives, matching CLI/server binaries, a verified
NATS executable, and the fixture) and runs
`scripts/verify-rust-test-package.sh` with no repository checkout, patching only
the three extracted public artifacts. Before the first `trellis-test` upload a
maintainer must confirm the crate name is available and owned by the publisher;
after publication, repeat the fixture smoke against the real crates.io versions
with no patch entries.

### Database schema baselines

Before the first release, each independently migrated schema has exactly one
initialization migration. Schema changes edit that baseline in place and use
fresh development and test databases; do not append migrations to carry an
earlier pre-release database forward, and older pre-release schema revisions are
unsupported. The migration runners, history/checksum validation, checks, and
baseline files are retained infrastructure, and ordinary restarts of a database
created from the current baseline keep their data without reapplying schema. At
the first release, freeze each shipped baseline; from then on, schema changes
add new ordered migrations instead of editing a shipped one.

## 1. Pick the release

Identify the previous release tag and choose the next version. For the first
OATS Center release (`0.100.0-rc.1`), there is no prior tag in the new
repository: use the upstream Qlever `main` merge-base commit as `--since`
when checking the initial changelog.

Set variables for the release you are cutting:

```sh
PREVIOUS_TAG=vX.Y.Z
RELEASE_VERSION=X.Y.Z
RELEASE_TAG="v$RELEASE_VERSION"
```

For a prerelease, include the suffix in `RELEASE_VERSION`:

```sh
PREVIOUS_TAG=vX.Y.Z
RELEASE_VERSION=X.Y.Z-rc.1
RELEASE_TAG="v$RELEASE_VERSION"
```

Examples only:

- stable: previous `vX.Y.Z`, new version `X.Y.Z`, tag `vX.Y.Z`
- prerelease: checked-in base `X.Y.Z`, prerelease version `X.Y.Z-rc.1`, tag `vX.Y.Z-rc.1`

Use stable version numbers without the `v` prefix when passing `--version`, `--from`, or `--to`. Use tag names with the `v` prefix when passing `--since` or `--tag`.

## 2. Update the changelog

`CHANGELOG.md` is the source of truth for both the repo changelog and the GitHub release body.

Review the commits and the actual code changes since the previous release before
finalizing the changelog:

```sh
/usr/bin/git log --oneline "$PREVIOUS_TAG..HEAD"
/usr/bin/git diff --stat "$PREVIOUS_TAG..HEAD"
/usr/bin/git diff "$PREVIOUS_TAG..HEAD"
```

For exact path-limited release evidence, use direct Git. Do not rely on
condensed wrapper output for evidence that must be copied into release notes,
approvals, or audit records:

```sh
/usr/bin/git diff -- <paths>
git diff -- <paths>
```

Use the code review, not just the commit subjects, to verify the changelog is
functionally complete and correct. It should cover user-visible changes,
migration concerns, fixed user-visible bugs, and notable operational details
without becoming a raw commit dump. Generated-file-only churn does not need its
own entry, but generated artifacts should point back to a covered source or
contract change.

Add a section whose heading matches the release version exactly:

- stable: `## [X.Y.Z] - YYYY-MM-DD`
- prerelease: `## [X.Y.Z-rc.1] - YYYY-MM-DD`

For a stable release, move the relevant `Unreleased` bullets into the new version section before pushing the release marker.

## 3. Bump checked-in base versions

First verify that release-managed versions are currently consistent:

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release check-versions
```

For a new stable base release, bump release-managed TypeScript and Rust manifests together:

```sh
CURRENT_BASE_VERSION=<current-base-version>
NEXT_BASE_VERSION=<next-base-version>
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release bump --from "$CURRENT_BASE_VERSION" --to "$NEXT_BASE_VERSION"
cargo xtask install --update
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release check-versions
```

`release bump` updates release-managed `deno.json`, `deno.npm.json`, `Cargo.toml`, and Trellis-owned `trellis.toml` manifests while preserving file layout. `cargo xtask install --update` refreshes their dependent IDL locks and generated SDKs. App and demo manifests that intentionally use `0.0.0` are skipped. For prereleases, run `release prepare --tag` and commit the RC versions and the matching `CHANGELOG.md` section to the candidate, so `Check` builds and verifies the exact release versions.

Do not run `release bump` just to add a prerelease suffix like `-rc.1`. Prerelease suffixes are handled from the tag by `release prepare` in the publish workflow.

## 4. Verify release metadata

Check that the changelog has the release section and review files changed since the previous tag:

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release changelog-check --version "$RELEASE_VERSION" --since "$PREVIOUS_TAG"
```

Run the release metadata verification:

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release check-metadata --version "$RELEASE_VERSION" --since "$PREVIOUS_TAG"
```

You can preview the GitHub release notes before pushing the release marker:

```sh
tmpdir=$(mktemp -d)
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release write-notes --tag "$RELEASE_TAG" --output "$tmpdir/release-notes.md"
less "$tmpdir/release-notes.md"
```

## 5. Confirm correctness and package locally

`Check` is the single authoritative verification pipeline: it builds the
release-profile runtime, every CLI target, and both image architectures;
packages the Rust and npm packages; runs the artifact-only package consumer; and
runs the formatting, lint/type, generated-freshness, and full live-integration
suites behind one acceptance gate. It runs for the candidate commit. `Release`
does not rebuild anything; it publishes the artifacts of the successful `Check`
run for that exact commit.

Keep the release commit limited to the release preparation listed below. If it
changes product behavior, return that change to `main`, obtain a new successful
`Check`, and rebuild the release commit from that base.

For focused package confidence before pushing the release marker, install once
and run the same package-specific checks used by the Release workflow:

```sh
cargo xtask install
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- protocol-wasm
cargo package --manifest-path Cargo.toml --workspace --allow-dirty \
  --exclude trellis-local-bootstrap --exclude trellis-bootstrap \
  --exclude trellis-local-nats --exclude trellis-jobs-runtime \
  --exclude trellis-events-runtime --exclude trellis-test \
  --exclude trellis-protocol-wasm --exclude trellis-codegen-ts \
  --exclude trellis-codegen-rust \
  --exclude trellis-cli --exclude trellis-runtime
deno task -c ts/deno.json packages:build:npm:installed
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:packaging:built
bash scripts/release-ts-dry-run.sh
```

Rust uses the current stable toolchain only. New workspace members must inherit
`[lints] workspace = true`; the normal `Check` workflow enforces that policy.

For focused local Deno loops after one repository install, use the prepared partitions instead of rerunning installation before every slice:

```sh
cargo xtask install
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:result
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:trellis
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:trellis-svelte
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:trellis-test
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:ui-tools
deno task -c ts/deno.json packages:build:npm
deno task -c ts/deno.json test:packaging:built
```

The prepared partitions are the non-integration Deno release-test set. Keep
TypeScript client-library integration coverage in `deno task -c ts/deno.json
test:integration` so live integration tests (which run NATS as a managed
binary) run only in the intended integration suite.

Also run repository installation when contracts, generated SDKs, runtime surfaces, or release packaging can be affected:

```sh
cargo xtask install
```

If the guide site changed, also run:

```sh
deno task -c docs/deno.json check
```

If packaging changed, add the relevant package or image smoke checks before committing. For example:

```sh
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- build --workspace --release
deno task -c ts/deno.json build:npm
(cd ts/packages/result && deno publish --dry-run --allow-slow-types --allow-dirty)
(cd ts/packages/trellis && deno publish --dry-run --allow-slow-types --allow-dirty)
(cd ts/packages/trellis-testkit && deno publish --dry-run --allow-slow-types --allow-dirty)
# The runtime image is assembly-only: stage the produced executables first.
goarch="$(case "$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/^host: //p')" in x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu) echo amd64;; aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu) echo arm64;; *) echo unknown;; esac)"
mkdir -p "dist/native/${goarch}"
cp target/release/trellis target/release/trellis-server "dist/native/${goarch}/"
docker build -f crates/runtime/Containerfile .
docker build -f web/Containerfile .
```

If the full integration suites cannot be run, record why and list the focused checks that did run.

## 6. Review the release commit

Make sure the release commit includes only the intended release preparation, typically:

- `CHANGELOG.md`
- release-managed version bumps for a stable release
- generated artifacts refreshed by `cargo xtask install`, if applicable
- docs, release tooling, or workflow fixes that must ship with the release

Then commit the release preparation:

```sh
git status
/usr/bin/git diff -- CHANGELOG.md Cargo.toml crates xtask ts web demos integration docs .github/workflows
git add CHANGELOG.md Cargo.toml crates xtask ts web demos integration docs .github/workflows
git commit -m "Prepare $RELEASE_TAG release"
```

Adjust the files and commit message to match what actually changed.

## 7. Push the release marker

Push the release commit to the matching release branch. The branch name is the release marker and must be `release/$RELEASE_TAG`:

```sh
git push origin HEAD:refs/heads/release/$RELEASE_TAG
```

For example, `RELEASE_TAG=v0.100.0-rc.1` pushes `release/v0.100.0-rc.1`.

The `Release` workflow fails early if the final release tag already exists. If the release gate fails, fix the release commit and push the release branch again; the version is not consumed because no tag has been created.

After the release gate passes, GitHub Actions creates an unsigned lightweight tag for `$RELEASE_TAG` on the marked commit and publishes:

- the GitHub release with changelog-backed notes
- npm packages
- crates.io crates
- GHCR images
- JSR packages

The unified `Release` workflow publishes in gated stages:

- `resolve-release` derives the final release tag from the `release/v*` branch and rejects already-used tags
- `prepare-release` resolves the successful `Check` run for the candidate commit, verifies release metadata, and writes release notes; the publish jobs download that run's verified artifacts
- `package-rust` verifies all publishable workspace crates, while `package-js` builds npm output from the prepared sources, checks the built package surfaces, runs npm and JSR publication dry-runs, and uploads the npm package artifact
- `build-targets-linux` builds the runtime executables once per native Linux architecture in a `rust:bookworm` container (matching the runtime image base) and uploads CLI archives; `build-targets-darwin` builds the macOS archives; both run inside `Check`. `verify-images` assembles the OCI archives from those prebuilt executables without compiling Trellis, and smokes the `trellis` image natively: `verify-images` runs the linux/amd64 smoke and `smoke-image-arm64` runs the linux/arm64 smoke. The smoke checks the baked nats-server version against `crates/local-nats/nats-binaries.json`, starts the image as its non-root user with a read-only config and writable state volume (`trellis-server --system all --local-nats=/usr/local/bin/nats-server`), waits for runtime readiness, stops it gracefully, and asserts no startup or write errors
- `create-release-tag` creates the release tag after the release gate passes
- `github-release` publishes CLI archives and checksums first
- `publish-crates`, `publish-npm`, and `publish-images` publish downstream artifacts after the GitHub release exists
- `publish-jsr` publishes the JSR package set after the GitHub release exists

The workflow is idempotent where practical: npm and crates.io publish steps skip versions that already exist. A registry publish is still not an atomic transaction across registries, so if a late publish step fails, inspect the workflow logs and rerun the workflow after fixing the underlying registry or workflow issue.

## 8. Verify the published release

Check that the GitHub release body matches the intended changelog section and that the expected artifacts were attached.

Then verify the downstream publishes you care about:

- npm packages have the expected version and dist-tag
- crates.io shows the expected crate versions
- GHCR images have the expected tags such as `$RELEASE_TAG`, `latest`, or `rc`
- the CLI archive checksums are present and match the published artifacts
- JSR packages have the expected version

## 9. Prepare for the next cycle

After publication, keep the `release/$RELEASE_TAG` branch. It remains the
durable base for hotfixes against that released line.

After a stable release, start the next development cycle by adding or reopening the `Unreleased` section in `CHANGELOG.md`.

If you want checked-in base versions to immediately move to the next line, do that in a separate follow-up change:

```sh
OLD_BASE_VERSION=<released-base-version>
NEW_BASE_VERSION=<next-base-version>
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release bump --from "$OLD_BASE_VERSION" --to "$NEW_BASE_VERSION"
cargo xtask install --update
cargo run --manifest-path xtask/Cargo.toml -- release check-versions
```
